"""Definition-first introductions for core lessons; rendered before existing detail."""
# chapter: section id: (definition, mechanism, example)
FOUNDATIONS = {
1: {
'mental-model': ('A computer network connects devices so they can exchange data using agreed rules called protocols. The Internet is a global interconnection of networks that communicate using the Internet Protocol (IP).', 'Hosts originate or consume application data; routers forward packets between networks. A protocol specifies message format, meaning, and the actions taken when messages arrive or timers expire.', 'When a laptop opens a website, the laptop and server are hosts; the intervening routers carry their packets without running the browser application.'),
'access': ('An access network connects an end system to the wider network. An Internet service provider (ISP) operates networks that provide Internet connectivity.', 'A local access link leads to provider networks. Providers exchange traffic through transit agreements or peering; the actual path follows connectivity and routing policy rather than a fixed number of hierarchy levels.', 'A home device can use Wi-Fi to reach its home gateway, then fiber to reach its ISP. Wi-Fi and fiber describe different parts of the same journey.'),
'delay': ('Delay is the time data takes to reach a specified point. Throughput is the rate successfully delivered at a specified layer. Packet loss occurs when a packet fails to arrive, often because of a drop or transmission error.', 'Processing, queueing, serialization, and propagation contribute different delays. A bottleneck limits sustained rate; buffers absorb temporary bursts but can increase waiting time.', 'A 10 Mb/s bottleneck cannot sustain a 100 Mb/s transfer merely because the sender has a 1 Gb/s local link. A faster link also does not remove long-distance propagation delay.'),
'layers': ('Layering organizes communication into responsibilities with defined interfaces. Encapsulation wraps higher-layer data in the headers, and sometimes trailers, required by lower-layer protocols; decapsulation interprets and removes those wrappers at the receiver.', 'An application message can become TCP data, then an IP payload, then an Ethernet payload. Each header serves its own scope; routers normally replace link framing while forwarding the IP packet.', 'For a web request, HTTP describes the request, TCP identifies the connection, IP identifies network endpoints, and Ethernet identifies the local next hop.'),
'security-history': ('Network security protects communication and systems against unauthorized access, alteration, disclosure, and disruption. A threat is a possible harmful action; a vulnerability is a weakness that makes an attack possible.', 'Defenses act at multiple places: authentication checks identities, encryption protects content, updates remove weaknesses, and filtering controls allowed traffic. No single layer protects every asset.', 'HTTPS can protect traffic in transit, but it cannot prevent malware on a laptop from reading data before encryption. Endpoint and network protection address different risks.')},
2: {
'processes': ('A process is a running instance of a program. A socket is an operating-system communication endpoint through which a process sends or receives network data.', 'A server commonly binds a socket to a local address and port. A client addresses that service; transport protocols deliver the data to the appropriate socket. A socket is software, not a physical port.', 'A browser process can maintain several connections to a web server. They can share the server port while using different connection tuples.'),
 'transport-choice': ('An application’s transport requirements describe the delivery behavior it needs: reliability, ordering, latency, throughput, and protection. TCP, UDP, and QUIC offer different combinations of mechanisms.', 'Choose based on what late, missing, or reordered data means to the application. UDP leaves more responsibilities to the application; TCP supplies an ordered byte stream; QUIC supplies secure streams over UDP.', 'A file download needs all bytes eventually. A live call may prefer to skip an old audio sample rather than delay later speech while recovering it.'),
'http': ('HTTP, the Hypertext Transfer Protocol, defines requests for resources and responses to those requests. A cache stores reusable responses; a cookie carries application state between appropriate requests.', 'A request supplies a method and target. The response supplies a status and metadata, with content when applicable. Cache rules decide reuse or revalidation; cookies and server records can identify a session despite stateless HTTP semantics.', 'A browser can revalidate a cached image. A 304 response permits reuse of the cached representation; it is not a new response body containing the image.'),
'quic': ('QUIC is a secure transport protocol carried in UDP datagrams. It provides connections containing independent streams, loss recovery, congestion control, and cryptographic protection.', 'QUIC endpoints handle functions that UDP does not supply. Stream independence prevents missing data in one stream from directly blocking delivery in another, although streams still share network capacity and connection-level controls.', 'HTTP/3 carries HTTP over QUIC. A missing packet containing one resource need not prevent an unrelated stream’s already-received data from being delivered.'),
'dns': ('The Domain Name System (DNS) is a distributed naming database that maps domain names to typed records. A resolver looks up records; an authoritative server publishes records for a zone.', 'A recursive resolver follows delegations when needed and caches answers for their allowed lifetime. Records can describe IPv4 addresses, IPv6 addresses, aliases, mail destinations, and other information.', 'Looking up an A record for a website name requests an IPv4 address. It does not look up the URL path or download the page itself.'),
'mail-cdn': ('Electronic mail is an application service that transfers and stores messages. A content delivery network (CDN) is a distributed set of delivery servers that brings content closer to users.', 'SMTP transfers mail between relevant clients and servers; mailbox-access protocols serve a different role. A CDN directs requests to suitable delivery locations and caches or retrieves content from an origin.', 'An email can remain in a mailbox while its recipient is offline. A popular video can be served from a nearby CDN cache instead of repeatedly crossing the entire path to its origin.'),
'streaming': ('Adaptive bitrate streaming delivers media in segments available at multiple quality levels. The player changes the selected representation as throughput and playback-buffer conditions change.', 'A manifest describes available media. The client downloads segments, buffers them, and balances visual quality against the risk of running out of playable data.', 'If a player estimates that a 6 Mb/s stream is unsustainable, it can request later segments at 3 Mb/s instead of repeatedly pausing playback.'),
'programming': ('Socket programming is the use of an operating system’s networking API to implement communicating applications. Client and server describe interaction roles, not necessarily different kinds of hardware.', 'A TCP server typically binds, listens, and accepts; a client connects. Both read and write the resulting stream. UDP sends and receives datagrams without a TCP-style accept operation.', 'An echo server receives data and sends it back. A TCP client must loop until its application-level message is complete because one read need not contain everything sent.')},
3: {
 'transport-model': ('The transport layer provides communication between application endpoints on hosts. Multiplexing combines traffic from multiple sockets for transmission; demultiplexing delivers incoming traffic to the appropriate socket.', 'Transport headers carry ports and protocol-specific state. IP gets data to a host interface; transport processing selects the application endpoint and applies the service its protocol defines.', 'A computer can browse the web while using DNS because the operating system distinguishes traffic by protocol, addresses, ports, and connection state.'),
'udp': ('UDP, the User Datagram Protocol, is a connectionless transport that sends separate messages called datagrams. It preserves message boundaries but does not itself guarantee arrival, ordering, or duplicate suppression.', 'An endpoint adds ports, a length, and a checksum to the message. Delivery uses IP; the application must handle missing messages or add recovery when needed.', 'A small DNS request can fit in one UDP datagram. If no answer arrives, the resolver may retry; UDP itself does not perform that retry.'),
'reliability': ('Reliable data transfer uses error detection and recovery to deliver data according to an agreed service. Automatic Repeat reQuest (ARQ) recovers from detected loss or damage using feedback and retransmission.', 'Stop-and-Wait permits one outstanding unit. Go-Back-N pipelines units and retransmits a range after loss in the textbook model; Selective Repeat selectively retransmits and buffers out-of-order units. Sequence numbers distinguish new data from duplicates.', 'If a receiver gets data but its ACK is lost, a sender may retransmit. The receiver needs sequence state to acknowledge again without delivering the same data twice.'),
 'tcp': ('TCP, the Transmission Control Protocol, provides a connection-oriented, reliable, ordered byte stream between endpoints. It does not preserve application message boundaries or guarantee a delivery deadline.', 'TCP numbers bytes, acknowledges receipt, detects loss, retransmits, and controls the amount in flight using receiver and congestion limits. An application still needs its own message framing and success semantics.', 'A 5000-byte application write may span several TCP segments. The receiver can obtain those bytes through multiple reads even when no data is lost.'),
'handshake': ('A TCP handshake establishes endpoint connection state, including initial sequence numbers and supported options. Connection termination closes the two directions of the byte stream.', 'The usual opening exchange is SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK. FIN signals that one sender has no more bytes; RST aborts a connection. This state is maintained by endpoints, not reserved bandwidth in every router.', 'One endpoint can finish sending a request with an orderly half-close while continuing to receive the reply in the other direction.'),
 'congestion': ('Congestion occurs when traffic demand exceeds available network service, causing queues and potentially loss. Congestion control adapts sending behavior to the network; flow control protects the receiver.', 'A sender uses a congestion window and possibly pacing to bound traffic in flight. Feedback such as loss, acknowledgments, or explicit marks affects its rate; the receiver advertises a separate receive window.', 'A receiver may have plenty of free memory while a shared router link is overloaded. Increasing the receive window does not solve that network bottleneck.'),
'evolution': ('ECN is explicit network congestion signaling. BBR is a family of congestion-control algorithms that model delivery rate and propagation time. QUIC is an extensible transport over UDP; these names describe different kinds of mechanisms.', 'ECN-capable equipment can mark traffic to signal congestion. Model-based senders estimate path conditions. QUIC moves transport evolution into endpoints while retaining congestion-control responsibilities.', 'A congestion mark can prompt a sender to reduce pressure before a queue overflows. It is a network signal, not evidence that an application has consumed its data.')},
4: {
'overview': ('The data plane performs actions on individual packets, such as forwarding, dropping, or marking. The control plane computes and installs the rules used for those actions.', 'On arrival, a device inspects relevant headers, selects a matching forwarding rule, and applies its action. Ordinary forwarding does not recompute the whole network’s routes for each packet.', 'A router can forward many packets using one installed prefix entry while a routing protocol separately reacts to a failed link.'),
'router': ('A router connects IP networks and forwards packets according to network-layer information. Its input ports, switching fabric, output ports, and control processor perform different parts of this job.', 'An input accepts a frame and performs lookup. The fabric moves the packet toward an output; queues hold it until transmission is possible. Control software maintains routes and device state.', 'Two packets arriving on different inputs for the same busy output must share its transmission capacity even when the internal fabric is fast.'),
'queues': ('A packet queue is temporary storage for traffic awaiting service. Scheduling decides which queued packet to serve next; buffer management decides what to admit, drop, or mark.', 'Queues form when arrivals temporarily outpace service. FIFO preserves queue order; priority and fair scheduling choose by other rules. Larger buffers absorb bursts but can also create long delays.', 'When several inputs feed a 100 Mb/s output at a combined 150 Mb/s, sustained demand exceeds service. Scheduling changes who waits; it cannot manufacture the missing capacity.'),
'ipv4': ('IPv4 is the Internet Protocol version that uses 32-bit addresses. A subnet groups addresses under a prefix; CIDR notation states the number of prefix bits explicitly.', 'An IPv4 header identifies source and destination and carries forwarding fields. A host or router compares the destination with route prefixes and selects the longest matching installed prefix.', '192.168.10.0/24 fixes the first 24 bits. It describes an address block, whereas 192.168.10.7 names one address within that block.'),
'nat-ipv6': ('NAT, Network Address Translation, rewrites addresses between address realms; NAPT also translates ports. IPv6 is a separate IP version with 128-bit addresses and a different header format.', 'A translator maintains or applies mappings for returning traffic. IPv6 expands addressing and changes protocol mechanisms; dual stack or translation can support coexistence. IPv6 is not simply NAT with longer addresses.', 'Two private clients can share one public IPv4 address using different translated ports. An IPv6 host may instead have a globally scoped address, still subject to firewall policy.'),
'match-action': ('Match–action forwarding selects packets by header fields or metadata and applies configured actions. It generalizes forwarding beyond a lookup based only on destination IP.', 'Rules can combine fields such as ingress port, protocol, address, and transport port. A matching rule can forward, drop, rewrite, count, or copy traffic; precedence resolves overlapping rules.', 'A rule may drop incoming TCP traffic to a protected service while permitting traffic to another destination. A separate counter records how many packets matched.'),
'architecture': ('The Internet’s hourglass architecture places a common IP layer between many applications and many link technologies. The end-to-end principle asks which functions can only be fully verified at the communicating endpoints.', 'Stable interfaces let applications and links evolve independently. Lower layers can assist with reliability or security, but only the application can verify some outcomes, such as a complete valid file.', 'Link error recovery can improve a wireless hop. A file-transfer application still needs to know that the full intended file reached the intended destination.')},
5: {
'overview': ('The control plane is the part of a network that determines forwarding behavior and installs the state needed to carry traffic. Routing finds paths; forwarding uses the resulting rules.', 'Distributed routers exchange protocol messages and calculate routes. An SDN design places some decisions in controller software. Both must keep installed state consistent with topology and policy changes.', 'After a link fails, control software finds an alternative and updates forwarding entries. Until convergence, some packets may be dropped or take an outdated path.'),
'algorithms': ('A routing algorithm computes paths using topology information, costs, or neighbor advertisements. Convergence means that routing state settles after a change, assuming the network and inputs stop changing.', 'Link-state algorithms use a topology view; distance-vector algorithms learn destination costs through neighbors. Their information exchange and failure behavior differ, even when both seek low-cost paths.', 'A router can learn that a neighbor offers a route of cost 4, add its own link cost 2, and obtain a candidate cost of 6. Stale neighbor information can make this estimate wrong.'),
'routing-lab': ('Dijkstra’s algorithm computes shortest paths from a source in a graph with nonnegative edge costs. A network graph represents routers as vertices and links as weighted edges.', 'It repeatedly settles the smallest tentative distance and relaxes outgoing edges, improving neighboring estimates. The resulting predecessor relationships form a shortest-path tree.', 'If A–B costs 2, B–C costs 3, and A–C costs 8, the path through B reaches C at cost 5. Lowest cost does not necessarily mean fewest hops.'),
'ospf': ('OSPF, Open Shortest Path First, is a link-state routing protocol used within a routing domain. An OSPF area limits the scope of detailed topology information.', 'Routers form neighbor relationships, exchange link-state information, and calculate paths from their area database. Areas and border routers allow hierarchical organization; costs describe routing preference within the protocol.', 'If one internal link’s cost increases, affected routers can calculate another lower-cost path after the updated advertisement is distributed.'),
'bgp': ('BGP, Border Gateway Protocol, exchanges IP-prefix reachability with path attributes. An autonomous system (AS) is a network or group of networks under a common routing administration and policy.', 'Routers apply import, selection, and export policies. eBGP operates between ASes; iBGP distributes BGP information inside an AS. Policy can prefer a route that is not geographically shortest.', 'An operator may prefer a customer route over a provider route for business reasons. Successfully receiving a BGP update does not prove the announced route is authorized.'),
'sdn': ('Software-defined networking (SDN) separates aspects of network-control logic from packet-forwarding devices and exposes programmable interfaces between them.', 'A controller combines network information and policy to install rules. The controller may be logically centralized while implemented by several machines for scale and resilience.', 'An application can request a traffic policy, and a controller can translate it into forwarding entries across multiple switches rather than requiring separate manual commands on each.'),
'icmp': ('ICMP is a network-layer protocol for control messages, diagnostics, and error reports associated with IP delivery. It is not an application transport like TCP or UDP.', 'Devices generate message types such as Echo Reply, Time Exceeded, and Destination Unreachable. Error messages quote information from the triggering packet so the sender can relate the failure to its traffic.', 'Traceroute varies TTL or Hop Limit and observes replies. A missing reply may mean filtering or rate limiting; it does not by itself prove that forwarding at that hop failed.'),
'management': ('Network management is the work of observing device state, changing configuration, detecting faults, and maintaining service. Telemetry is measured operational data exported for analysis.', 'Management systems collect counters, events, and configuration state, then compare them with intended behavior. Protocols and APIs support monitoring and controlled updates; validation checks the actual result.', 'Rising interface-drop counters can identify a congested output. A successful configuration command alone does not prove that the user’s application now works.')},
6: {
'service': ('The data-link layer delivers frames over a link or bridged link domain. A frame is a protocol-defined wrapper containing payload and information for local delivery and error detection.', 'Adapters frame data, address it where the technology requires, and transmit using the local medium’s rules. Different hops may use different link protocols while carrying the same IP packet.', 'A laptop can send over Wi-Fi to an access point while an onward segment uses Ethernet. The IP service spans both even though their frame formats differ.'),
'errors': ('Error detection adds redundancy so a receiver can identify some corrupted data. Error correction uses sufficient redundancy to recover from certain errors without a retransmission.', 'Parity, checksums, and cyclic redundancy checks (CRCs) detect different error patterns. Recovery may discard and retransmit a damaged frame or use forward error correction. Detection is not a guarantee against every error.', 'Ethernet’s FCS can flag a corrupted frame. It does not itself resend the frame or authenticate that the sender is trustworthy.'),
'access': ('A multiple-access protocol coordinates senders that share a transmission medium. It decides when each may transmit to reduce or resolve conflicting use of that medium.', 'Channel partitioning assigns resources, random access uses contention, and taking-turns methods coordinate opportunities. Efficiency and delay depend on load, overhead, and the medium.', 'Two wireless stations may both sense idle and start transmitting close together. Backoff and recovery help them retry, but do not make collisions impossible.'),
'ethernet': ('Ethernet is a family of wired LAN technologies that carries link-layer frames. ARP maps an IPv4 next-hop address to a local link-layer address on networks such as Ethernet.', 'A sender determines the IP next hop, resolves its MAC address when necessary, and sends the frame. The remote IP destination and the local Ethernet destination have different scopes.', 'To reach a remote web server, a host normally resolves its gateway’s MAC address, not the distant server’s MAC address.'),
'switch-lab': ('An Ethernet switch bridges frames between ports using a forwarding database indexed by MAC address and VLAN context. Learning builds this database from observed source addresses.', 'The switch records where sources arrived, then looks up destinations. Flooding is confined to eligible ports in the relevant VLAN; spanning-tree and other policy can restrict forwarding.', 'After learning that host A is on port 1, the switch can send frames for A directly there. An unknown destination may first require flooding.'),
'virtualization': ('Network virtualization creates logical networks over shared infrastructure. A VLAN separates a bridged domain, MPLS forwards using labels, and VXLAN carries an overlay Ethernet network across an IP underlay.', 'These mechanisms change how traffic is classified or encapsulated. Their scope and overhead differ; an overlay still depends on working underlay connectivity and adequate packet-size budgets.', 'Two tenant networks can use separate logical segments on the same physical fabric. Isolation must be configured correctly; shared hardware alone does not establish it.'),
'datacenter': ('A data-center network interconnects servers, storage, and services within or between facilities. A leaf-spine fabric provides many short paths between server-facing leaf switches.', 'Equal-cost multipath can spread flows across paths. Load balancers distribute application requests or connections among replicas, while queues and congestion control handle competing traffic.', 'Many workers responding to one server at once can overload its receiving link even when the overall fabric has substantial capacity. This many-to-one pattern is called incast.'),
'journey': ('An end-to-end web request is a sequence of cooperating protocols, each solving a different part of delivery. Configuration, naming, local forwarding, transport, security, and application exchange are separate tasks.', 'A host obtains configuration, resolves the server name when needed, reaches its next hop, and establishes the required transport and security state before exchanging HTTP messages. Cached state can skip some exchanges.', 'A working Wi-Fi connection does not prove that DNS or HTTPS works. Diagnose the step that fails instead of treating every failure as loss of Internet connectivity.')},
7: {
'wireless': ('Wireless networking transmits information using electromagnetic waves rather than a guided cable between each communicating pair. Mobility means a device changes its point of attachment or location while requiring service.', 'Receivers cope with noise, interference, attenuation, and multipath. Shared access and changing signal quality affect delivery; mobility adds discovery, handover, and reachability decisions.', 'A stationary laptop on Wi-Fi is wireless but not moving. A device that moves between attachment points has mobility concerns in addition to radio transmission concerns.'),
'physical': ('Modulation maps information to signal variations. Channel coding adds structured redundancy for error recovery. A modulation and coding scheme selects a combination suited to channel conditions.', 'More distinguishable symbols can carry more bits per symbol but need better signal quality. Added redundancy consumes capacity while improving recovery under suitable error conditions.', 'When a device moves farther from an access point, it may switch to a lower-rate, more robust scheme to maintain communication rather than keep a fast but unreliable one.'),
'wifi': ('Wi-Fi is wireless LAN networking based on IEEE 802.11. An access point connects associated stations to a distribution system; association is not the same as obtaining IP configuration or Internet access.', 'Stations discover a network, perform the required access/security exchanges, and contend or follow applicable scheduling rules for airtime. Local acknowledgments and retries assist frame delivery.', 'A phone may associate successfully with an access point but still fail to obtain a DHCP lease. Radio association and network-layer configuration are distinct stages.'),
'ran': ('A radio access network (RAN) connects user devices to a mobile operator’s core through base stations. In 5G, the gNodeB is the base-station role and UE means user equipment.', 'The base station schedules shared radio resources using channel feedback and service requirements. Coding, modulation, and retransmission adapt delivery to radio conditions.', 'Two phones near the same base station may obtain different rates because their signal conditions, demand, and scheduling allocations differ.'),
'core': ('The mobile core manages subscriber connectivity and carries user traffic between the radio network and external data networks. Control-plane functions manage sessions and mobility; user-plane functions forward traffic.', 'Authentication and session procedures authorize connectivity and create forwarding state. Separating control from user traffic allows these responsibilities to scale and be placed differently.', 'A phone’s application data can pass through a user-plane function while separate signaling manages its session. The signaling is not the application payload.'),
'mobility': ('Mobility management maintains reachability as a device changes attachment points. Handover transfers an active connection or service context to another access point or base station.', 'The system updates radio or forwarding state and may use anchors or tunnels to preserve continuity. Whether an IP address changes depends on the network design and boundary crossed.', 'Walking between coverage areas during a call may trigger a handover. Switching to an unrelated Wi-Fi network can also change the IP path and disrupt sessions.'),
'other': ('Wireless technologies choose different tradeoffs among range, power use, rate, cost, and topology. A short-range peripheral link and a long-lived remote sensor do not have the same requirements.', 'Designers choose spectrum, duty cycle, channel access, and protocol overhead for the workload. Sleeping saves energy but can increase the time before a device can receive data.', 'A battery sensor sending a small reading every hour may favor low energy use over video-rate throughput; a headset instead needs timely continuous audio.')},
8: {
'goals': ('Confidentiality limits disclosure; integrity protects against unauthorized alteration; availability keeps a service usable. Authentication checks a claimed identity, while authorization decides what that identity may do.', 'A threat model identifies assets, adversaries, and trust boundaries. Security mechanisms must address the stated threats and be combined with operational controls.', 'A correctly authenticated user can still lack permission to read a file. Encrypting that file in transit does not decide access rights.'),
'crypto': ('Symmetric cryptography uses shared secret-key material. Public-key cryptography uses a related public/private key pair for operations such as signatures or key establishment, depending on the algorithm.', 'Protocols commonly use public-key mechanisms to establish trust or keys, then symmetric authenticated encryption for bulk data. Algorithms, key handling, and protocol design all affect security.', 'Knowing a server’s public key can help verify its signature. It does not give the verifier the private key or permission to impersonate the server.'),
'integrity': ('A cryptographic hash maps data to a fixed-size digest. A message authentication code uses a secret key to authenticate data. A digital signature uses a private signing key and public verification key.', 'A digest alone cannot prove origin because an attacker can replace both data and digest. A MAC authenticates among secret holders; signatures can be checked without sharing the signing secret.', 'A file hash from the same compromised download server is not independent proof of authenticity. A valid signature is useful only when the verifying key is trusted for that purpose.'),
'authentication': ('Authentication establishes evidence that a peer controls the credentials associated with an identity. Replay is reuse of an earlier valid message; a man-in-the-middle attack interposes between communicating peers.', 'Fresh challenges, transcript binding, and authenticated key establishment prevent specific reuse or substitution attacks. Authorization and secure credential storage remain separate requirements.', 'A fresh random challenge can make yesterday’s response unusable today, provided the response securely binds the challenge to the intended identity and protocol exchange.'),
'email': ('End-to-end secure email protects message content for intended recipients and can authenticate the sender’s signed content. This differs from encrypting only a connection between two mail servers.', 'Hybrid encryption uses a symmetric content key and protects that key for recipients. Signatures let recipients check integrity and signing-key ownership, subject to their trust in the key binding.', 'A mail server using TLS may still store readable messages. Content encryption can limit what intermediaries can read, although some metadata remains visible.'),
 'tls': ('TLS is a protocol for authenticating peers and protecting application communication against eavesdropping and tampering. HTTPS combines HTTP with a secure transport using TLS-related mechanisms.', 'A handshake negotiates parameters, authenticates as configured, and derives traffic keys. Record protection then encrypts and authenticates application bytes; certificate validation checks the expected service identity.', 'A valid certificate for one domain does not authenticate a different domain. The client must validate both trust and the requested server name.'),
'ipsec': ('IPsec is a suite of mechanisms for protecting IP traffic. A VPN creates a logical private connection over another network; a tunnel carries an inner packet within an outer packet.', 'Security associations define protection state. ESP can protect content and integrity according to its configuration; tunnel mode wraps an inner IP packet. Key establishment and policy select which traffic is protected.', 'A site-to-site VPN can protect traffic between gateways. Protection outside those tunnel endpoints depends on the remaining protocols and network policy.'),
'wireless-security': ('Wireless access security authenticates participants and protects communication over the wireless access link. Its scope is different from end-to-end application security.', 'Access protocols establish keys and enforce admission policy. Wi-Fi and cellular systems use different mechanisms; protection on the radio hop does not automatically protect every later hop.', 'A password-protected Wi-Fi link can encrypt the local radio traffic while HTTPS separately protects a connection from the browser to the website.'),
'operations': ('A firewall enforces traffic policy. Intrusion detection identifies suspicious activity; intrusion prevention may block it. Segmentation limits communication between defined network or workload groups.', 'Rules, inspection, logs, patching, and response procedures work together. Visibility has limits, especially with encrypted traffic, and alerts require interpretation rather than automatic assumptions of compromise.', 'Separating a guest network from an administrative network reduces direct reachability. It does not replace authentication, updates, or application-level access controls.')}
}
